        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Splitting the middle term"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A rectangular plot has area 528 square metres and its length is one more than twice its breadth. Finding the breadth leads to a quadratic equation."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "(2x - 3)(x - 1)",
          "ax^2 + bx + c = 0",
          "form the equation",
          "reject impossible roots",
          "root = zero",
          "split the middle term",
          "verify",
          "x = 3/2, 1"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "A quadratic equation in x has the standard form a x squared + b x + c = 0 where a is not zero.\nA number alpha is a root if a alpha squared + b alpha + c equals 0. The roots of the equation are the zeroes of the polynomial.\nTo factorise, split the middle term. For 2x squared - 5x + 3 we need two numbers with product 6 and sum -5. They are -2 and -3.\nWrite 2x squared - 2x - 3x + 3 = 2x(x - 1) - 3(x - 1) = (2x - 3)(x - 1).\nSet each factor to zero: x = 3/2 or x = 1. These are the two roots.\nBefore solving a word problem, write the situation as an equation in standard form, then factorise.\nAlways reject a root that does not make sense, such as a negative length or a fractional number of people.\nCheck each root by substituting it into the original equation."
        }
      },
